+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole number of seconds,
+like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` for
+days, `h` for hours, `m` for minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit may
+appear at most once and in that order, without spaces. Units are
+case-insensitive, so `1H30M` is the same as `1h30m`.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    import datetime
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(),
+                  default=datetime.timedelta(minutes=5))
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():.0f} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=30m1h'])
+```
+
+A {class}`datetime.timedelta` value, such as the default above, is passed
+through unchanged.
+
